# P25K_G's CBIOS and PBIOS - the older PHILIPS P2000B generation, and the
# only sample of it this project has. It is also the disk with the most
# software on it, which is why it is worth naming.
#
#   ./p2500-emu --disk disks/P25K_G.raw --symbols symbols/p25k_g.sym \
#     --max-steps 3000000 --disasm EDDC:12
#   p2500-gui --disk disks/P25K_G.raw --symbols symbols/p25k_g.sym --panels disasm
#
# NOT symbols/cpm.sym plus an offset. Both SYSCBI.PHI files load at $E200,
# but G's ends at $E833 where P25K_B's ends at $EABE, and its CBIOS is a
# different implementation - the two diverge at the first instruction of
# CONIN. Its SYSPBI.PHI loads at $EB80 against B's $EAC0, the +$C0 the
# research notes record, but inside that module the routines have drifted
# by their own amounts: +$AF, +$BB, +$83, +$31, and +$90 all appear below.
#
# How each name here was established, so it can be re-checked or refuted:
#
#   - the driver layer, from G's own device table and function tables,
#     matched to B's by device id and function code (tools/sym_check.py's
#     companion, tools/port_syms.py --devtable, in the research repo)
#   - the interrupt handlers, from G's IM2 trampolines, which are the only
#     place those addresses appear
#   - the rest of PBIOS, by walking both builds' disassembly in lockstep
#     from those entry points: every name below had at least two
#     instructions whose operands say so, or was confirmed by hand
#   - the CBIOS half, read end to end against the same dump taken from
#     P25K_B. The 17 jump-table slots and their bodies are derived from
#     page zero at run time and are deliberately absent here; what this
#     file names is everything BETWEEN them. There is no IN and no OUT
#     anywhere in $E2B2-$E629 - this CBIOS is a pure client of PBIOS's
#     request layer - so every device access is `ld de,<block> / call
#     REQ.SUBMIT`, and the eight static request blocks below are what
#     made the module readable. Write-up: p25k_g-cbios.md in the
#     research repo
#   - three tables, by content: the baud table and the sector-translate
#     table are byte-identical to B's, and the escape table has the same
#     {code, pointer} records
#
# Four names in cpm.sym have NO counterpart here, which is a finding, not
# a gap in this file: TICKS (G's clock ISR has no counter block at all -
# so nothing in RAM ticks at 50 Hz on this disk, and the field-sync trick
# in demos/p2500.inc does not work), KEYXLAT.TAB (no key translation
# table - no cursor diamond, no dead keys), ESC.COUNT and CONOUT.XLAT
# (G's CONOUT only posts a request; the screen driver owns the escapes).
#
# And three structures here have no counterpart in cpm.sym, for the same
# reason: BANNER.REQ (G's CBIOS prints "PHILIPS P2000B / CP/M 2.2 - 58K"
# on a cold boot; B's prints nothing - its banner comes from the loader
# and is not resident once CP/M is up), DPB.ALT (a second, 1K-block 132K
# format B does not carry), and the whole DRI blocking/deblocking
# variable block at $E748-$E758, which is a different disk
# implementation rather than a relocation of B's.
#
# Cross-checked the way cpm.sym is (tools/sym_check.py in the research
# repo, 16-byte windows over $E200-$FFFF), with a second run of the SAME
# disk as the control:
#
#   P25K_G run 2   140 of 143 same address, 0 moved - so these names are
#                  stable, not artefacts of one run. The 3 that do not
#                  compare are live data (HSTBUF, KBD.RING, GENQ.BUF)
#   P25K_B         3 same, 9 moved, 128 absent - which is the measurement
#                  behind "a different implementation, not a relocation"
#
# Fingerprint, so `--symbols auto` can recognise this build without
# being told which disk is in the drive:
# the same window over P25K_G's own, different, CBIOS build.
# match E200:0400:E2D1C08D
#
# And the disk itself, recognisable before anything has booted - the CP/M
# directory at $2000. See symbols/cpm.sym for why it has to be the
# directory rather than the boot track.
# match-disk 2000:0800:ECEBB62C P25K_G - PHILIPS P2000B games disk
#
# Format: ADDR NAME, hex, one per line. $ or 0x prefix optional, : or =
# accepted in place of the space, # and ; start a comment.

# ---- CP/M itself: the same Digital Research build as every other disk
#      here, byte-identical where it is code
CC00 CCP
D400 BDOS.base
E086 BDOS.RESET
E117 BDOS.RDROVEC
E1AD RO.VECTOR
E1AF LOGIN.VECTOR
E1C1 BDOS.DPBCOPY   ; BDOS's own copy of the DPB - at $E1C1 in both builds

# ---- CBIOS: disk, from G's SELDSK ($E469: drive*16 + $E233)
E233 DPH.TABLE      ; stride 16; DPH[0] = XLT $E253, DIRBUF, DPB, CSV, ALV
E253 SECTRAN.TAB    ; byte-identical to B's: 01 02 05 06 09 0A 0D 0E ...
E273 DPB            ; SPT 32, BSH/BLM 4/15, EXM 1, DSM 149, DRM 63,
                    ; CKS 16, OFF 2 - and AL0 $C0 where B has $80
E291 DRIVE.MAP      ; two entries ($10 $10): this BIOS knows two drives
E2A7 DRIVEMAP.PTR   ; SELDSK reads the map through this pointer
E74B DRIVE.CUR      ; SELDSK stores the selected drive here
E74C TRACK.CUR      ; SETTRK stores the track here
E76B DIRBUF         ; from DPH[0]
E7FF ALV.A          ; from DPH[0]
E823 CSV.A          ; from DPH[0]

# ---- CBIOS: console
E2AF REQ.SUBMIT     ; one instruction, jp REQ.POST - CBIOS's door into PBIOS
E390 CONIN.body     ; also derived live; here for when page zero is not CP/M's
E3A8 CONIN.MASK     ; ld a,(CAPS.MASK) / and b
E3AD CAPS.CHECK     ; polls KBD.REQ's status, then toggles the mask
E3B9 CAPS.TOGGLE    ; xor $20 into CAPS.MASK
E3C3 CAPS.MASK      ; $DF as shipped, and ANDed with the input - so this
                    ; BIOS CLAMPS to upper case where B's XOR $20 SWAPS
                    ; case. Verified by typing: `DIR` echoes `DIR` here
                    ; and `dir` on P25K_B
E3C4 KBD.REQ        ; the console-in request block
E3C6 KBD.REQ.STAT   ; +2 of the block: CONIN spins on this reaching 0
E3C8 KBD.REQ.DATA   ; +4: the returned code. $80 is not a character -
                    ; it is the capitals-lock key, and CAPS.CHECK eats it
E3C9 KBD.REQ2       ; a second block for device $30, posted by BOOT

# ---- PBIOS: the request and driver layer
EB80 REQ.POST       ; B $EAC0 +$C0 - post a request, then spin on its status
EC16 DEV.TABLE      ; count byte, then {id, ptr_lo, ptr_hi}; ids $31 $30
                    ; $00 $01 $40 $FF - two disk units, where B has four
EC46 DRV.DISPATCH   ; every driver's `ld hl,fntab / call here`
EC9D EI.RETI        ; how every handler releases the daisy chain
ECA0 RING.PUSH      ; circular buffer push
ECB6 RING.POP       ; and pop
ECE6 DEVFF.DRV      ; device $FF, purpose unidentified (B $EC2B)
ECFB DEVFF.FNTAB

# ---- PBIOS: keyboard (device $30)
ED5A KBD.DRV
ED76 KBD.FNTAB      ; f00 $ED84, f01 $EDB1, f03 $EDBE, f04 $EDCB
EDB1 KBD.RINGINIT
EDD9 CTC3.ISR       ; keyboard byte-ready, from the IM2 trampoline at $FF2A
EDDC CTC3.ISR.body  ; in a,($06) at $EDE7
EE38 KBD.REQ.PTR    ; the driver's current request
EE3E KBD.RING       ; header [count][write][read], 32 bytes at +3

# ---- PBIOS: screen (device $31)
EE84 SCREEN.DRV
EEAB SCREEN.FNTAB   ; f00 $EEB9, f01 $EEDE, f03 $EEF7, f05 $EF1D
EF1D SCREEN.WRITE   ; function $05
F1FB ESC.TAB        ; {code, pointer} records, same shape as B's
F224 ESC.TAB2       ; the alternate set
F4B6 SCREEN.REQ.PTR
F4B8 SCREEN.FLAGS   ; bit 2 drives the generated-code queue below
F4BE ATTR.CUR       ; current attribute; its low nibble reaches port $0A
F4D7 ATTR.TMPL      ; 3E 00 D3 0A - the OUT ($0A) template
F4EF CRTC.R10TMPL   ; the R10 write template used by ESC C / ESC c
F4F8 GENQ.WPTR      ; write pointer into the generated-code queue
FD54 GENQ.BUF       ; the queue itself, terminated $C9 and then called

# ---- PBIOS: clock
F40F CTC2.ISR       ; 50 Hz field strobe, from the trampoline at $FF33
F412 CTC2.ISR.body  ; NO tick counter here - see the header

# ---- PBIOS: serial / printer (device $40)
F53C SER.DRV
F558 SER.FNTAB      ; f00 $F569, f01 $F5BC, f03 $F5D7, f04 $F60C, f05 $F5EB
F5C9 BAUD.TAB       ; 7 x {control, time constant}, byte-identical to B's
F61A CTC0.ISR       ; transmit clock
F629 TX.WAIT        ; waits on port $05 bit 6
F6EC CTC1.ISR       ; receive sampler
F7AD TX.FLAGS
F7B0 TX.TIMEOUT
F7B2 SER.REQ.PTR

# ---- PBIOS: disk (devices $00 and $01)
F82A DISK.DRV
F858 DISK.FNTAB     ; nine functions, same codes as B's
FBCC DMA.ISR        ; from the trampoline at $FF21
FC4C PIOA.ISR       ; from the trampoline at $FF18

# ---- The IM2 layer, at I=$FF
FF18 IM2.TRAMP      ; six trampolines, $FF18 to $FF45, vector order
FF63 ISR.SHIM       ; saves A, patches its own jp with the handler address
FFC0 CBIOS.IM2      ; vectors $C0-$CB: CTC 0-3, PIO A, DMA
FFDE ISR.SAVE.A
FFDF ISR.SAVE.HL

# ---- Derived straight from this machine's own tables by
#      tools/derive_syms.py (research repo), which needs no
#      reference build: the per-drive check and allocation
#      vectors out of each DPH, and every driver's function
#      entries out of its function table. Function codes are
#      the driver's own; $05 is write on the screen driver.
E7EB ALV.B
E813 CSV.B
ED03 DEVFF.F00
ED3E DEVFF.F03
ED84 KBD.F00
EDBE KBD.F03
EDCB KBD.F04
EEB9 SCREEN.F00
EEDE SCREEN.F01
EEF7 SCREEN.F03
F569 SER.F00
F5BC SER.F01
F5D7 SER.F03
F5EB SER.F05
F60C SER.F04
F875 DISK.F00
F8BB DISK.F01
F8C3 DISK.F02
F8F6 DISK.F03
F91B DISK.F06
F923 DISK.F07
F92F DISK.F04
F93E DISK.F05
F94D DISK.F08

# ---- CBIOS: the request-block layout, which is what this CBIOS is made of
#      Every device access in $E2B2-$E629 is `ld de,<block> / call
#      REQ.SUBMIT`. There is no IN and no OUT anywhere in this CBIOS - it
#      is a pure client of PBIOS's request layer. Block header:
#        +0 function  +1 device id  +2 status  +3 data length  +4.. data
E33E BANNER.REQ      ; [05][31][00][20] + "PHILIPS P2000B\r\nCP/M 2.2 - 58K\r\n"
E342 BANNER.TEXT     ; 32 bytes - and an in-CBIOS statement of the build size
E3DA CONOUT.REQ      ; [05][31][00][01] - the one-character screen write
E3DE CONOUT.CHAR     ; +4: CONOUT stores C here, then submits
E405 PRINTER.MSG     ; "ACTIVATE PRINTER AND PRESS RETURN OR SPACE TO IGNORE"
E44A LIST.REQ        ; [05][40][00][01] - one character to device $40
E44E LIST.CHAR       ; +4: LIST stores C here
E45D LISTST.REQ      ; [03][40][00][00] - printer status
E461 LISTST.DATA     ; +4: non-zero means ready
E62A DISK.IO.REQ     ; [04|05][unit][status][00] then +4 track, +5 physical
                     ; sector, +6 count, +8 DMA ($E648). Submitted twice at
                     ; most: one retry, then ERFLAG
E634 DISK.SEL.REQ    ; [01][unit] + an 18-byte drive-parameter block, issued
                     ; before every host transfer. Only +1 (the unit) and
                     ; +16 (the DRIVE.MAP byte) are rewritten per call
E644 DISK.SEL.TYPE   ; +16: the drive type copied out of DRIVE.MAP

# ---- CBIOS: the paths between the entry points. The 17 jump-table slots
#      and their bodies are derived from page zero at run time and are
#      deliberately absent; these are the internals those bodies share.
E2A1 DPB.ALT.PTR     ; -> $E282. Read by NOTHING resident: a patch point
E2A3 DPB.PTR         ; -> $E273, likewise unread
E2A5 DPHTAB.PTR      ; -> $E233, likewise - SELDSK uses an immediate instead
E2A9 CCP.JP          ; jp $CC00; BOOT ends with it, and its operand doubles
                     ; as the CCP load address WBOOT reads as a word
E2AC BDOS.JP         ; jp $D406; WBOOT calls page zero's BDOS through it
E282 DPB.ALT         ; a complete second 15-byte DPB: SPT 32, 1K blocks
                     ; (BSH/BLM 3/7), DSM 131, DRM 63, AL0 $C0, CKS 16,
                     ; OFF 2 - a 132K, 35-track format. No counterpart on
                     ; P25K_B, and nothing on this disk can select it
E313 BOOT.COMMON     ; where BOOT and WBOOT join: build page zero's jps,
                     ; SETDMA $0080, then jp CCP.JP with C = ($0004)
E362 SYSCPM.FCB      ; [01]"SYSCPM  PHI" - WBOOT reloads the CCP/BDOS by
                     ; asking BDOS to open and read this file, 17 records
                     ; to $CC00. Not a system-track read
E479 SELDSK.DPH      ; drive*16 + $E233; also called by READ and HSTWRITE
E3EE LIST.PROMPT     ; the printer-not-ready dialogue, driven by CONIN
E3FF LIST.DISABLE    ; writes $C9 over $E3DF - SPACE patches LIST's own
                     ; entry point to RET for the rest of the session, and
                     ; a warm boot does not undo it because WBOOT reloads
                     ; SYSCPM.PHI only. Cold boot is the only way back
E4D1 RWSETUP         ; READ's tail: readop=1, rsflag=1, wrtype=2
E4E5 WRITE.PREP      ; WRITE's head: wrtype=C, and on wrtype 2 (write to an
                     ; unallocated block) prime UNACNT=16 - which is
                     ; 2048/128, so it is the block size restated
E53B UNASEC.NEXT     ; advances UNASEC by searching SECTRAN.TAB for the
                     ; current entry and taking the one after it - the skew
                     ; table used as a successor function. The track-wrap
                     ; case above it falls into this and overwrites its own
                     ; UNASEC=1 (the `jr c` at $E539 can never be taken
                     ; after `cp $20`), so write-ahead is simply lost at a
                     ; track boundary and the safe pre-read takes over
E55B RWCOMMON        ; HSTSEC = (sector-1)>>1, then the host-buffer
                     ; compare, the 128-byte LDIR, and the write-through
E5F5 HSTREAD         ; function $04 on the drive's own device id
E5F9 HSTWRITE        ; function $05
E648 HSTBUF          ; 256 bytes, $E648-$E747 - so a physical sector is 256
                     ; bytes and holds two CP/M records

# ---- CBIOS: the blocking/deblocking state. These are Digital Research's
#      published sample names: this CBIOS is that skeleton, with the host
#      buffer at $E648 and a 2048-byte allocation block.
E748 HSTSEC          ; physical sector = (CP/M sector - 1) >> 1
E749 DMAADDR         ; SETDMA stores BC here
E74D SECTOR.CUR      ; SETSEC; 1-based and already translated
E74E HSTACT          ; host buffer holds something
E74F HSTWRT          ; host buffer is dirty
E750 UNACNT          ; records left in the unallocated block, primed to 16
E751 UNADRV
E752 UNATRK
E753 UNASEC          ; held translated, not logical - see UNASEC.NEXT
E754 ERFLAG          ; $FF after both tries of DISK.IO.REQ fail
E755 RSFLAG          ; the host sector must be pre-read
E756 READOP
E757 WRTYPE          ; BDOS's C: 0 normal, 1 directory, 2 unallocated
E758 DIRSEC          ; NOT a DRI name. READ sets it when the sector asked
                     ; for is track OFF, sector 1 - the first directory
                     ; sector - and RWCOMMON then skips the host-buffer
                     ; tag compare, forcing a physical re-read. With no
                     ; media-change line, this is how a swapped disk is
                     ; noticed at the top of every directory scan
E769 STACK.TOP       ; BOOT and WBOOT both set SP here
